WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.230
Last question today, we sort of alluded to
it in the first, uh, in the second question

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:08.670
is the battle between social democracy and
democratic socialism.

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:09.670
One worth having.

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:12.240
So the, there are two answers to this.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:17.190
Ideologically, there is a major difference
between a social Democrat who believes in

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:20.160
a capitalist system that is well-regulated.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:28.410
The coroners are sort of sanded off to make
the system work better for more people.

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:31.050
It sets a floor, it sets a ceiling.

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:35.440
We've talked about social democracy, that
versus socializing the means of production

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:37.019
as an actual socialist.

00:00:37.019 --> 00:00:39.329
There is a world of difference between those
two ideas.

00:00:39.329 --> 00:00:43.960
So the debate between social democracy and
democratic socialism is not just worth having.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:48.949
It's an incredibly important debate that separates
drastically different world views.

00:00:48.949 --> 00:00:50.979
That's the theoretical answer.

00:00:50.979 --> 00:00:59.429
Now, in modern American politics, we are not
at risk or we don't have the possibility of

00:00:59.429 --> 00:01:02.260
going to democratic socialism anytime soon.

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:06.450
I've said this before, when Donald Trump gets
up in front of a, you know, the state of the

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:10.920
union and during the state of the union address
and says, we will never be a socialist country.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:14.430
First of all, he doesn't know that things
can happen over 50 years.

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:18.329
You know, over 30 years, over a hundred years,
that Donald Trump simply can't predict.

00:01:18.329 --> 00:01:24.100
So it's a dumb thing to say, but there's also
no risk that we're about to become a socialist

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:25.100
country.

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:29.630
The serious reforms that are being discussed
right now around healthcare, around inequality,

00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:35.859
around education, public transit, housing,
all of these things, the risk is that we go

00:01:35.859 --> 00:01:38.799
slightly more in the direction of social democracy.

00:01:38.799 --> 00:01:44.179
And so I believe that in in an academic sense,
in a theoretical sense, we should be discussing

00:01:44.179 --> 00:01:49.789
social democracy versus democratic socialism
in the immediate, the goals for both are probably

00:01:49.789 --> 00:01:50.789
the same.

00:01:50.789 --> 00:01:54.799
If you are a social Democrat, you want to
get to universal healthcare coverage maybe

00:01:54.799 --> 00:01:58.180
through something like Medicare for all or
other types of plans that I've mentioned.

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:03.479
If you are a democratic socialist, that still
is the next step for healthcare, which gets

00:02:03.479 --> 00:02:06.619
you closer to what you ultimately want.

00:02:06.619 --> 00:02:08.560
It's an academic debate right now.

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:12.799
It may become a practical debate at some point
in the future in the United States, but we're

00:02:12.799 --> 00:02:15.590
not becoming a democratic socialist country
anytime soon.

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:22.030
There, uh, effectively, no, almost no democratic
socialists in positions of power.

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:26.409
Even some of them who call themselves democratic
socialists are advocating policies of social

00:02:26.409 --> 00:02:27.409
democracy.

00:02:27.409 --> 00:02:32.830
So have the debate, but the next steps are
very similar, whether you're a social Democrat

00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:34.140
or a democratic socialist.

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:38.599
And I hope that we can unite among making
those accomplishments a reality.

